WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.802
Machine learning models often look excellent on
benchmark datasets,

00:00:04.882 --> 00:00:08.203
then break the moment they hit real production data.

00:00:08.283 --> 00:00:13.565
This paper by Matthew Groh at the MIT Media Lab
argues that the usual explanation,

00:00:13.645 --> 00:00:16.116
distribution shift, is the wrong frame.

00:00:16.196 --> 00:00:21.479
Distribution shift only measures that two datasets
differ; it never tells you why.

00:00:21.559 --> 00:00:23.441
Groh introduces context shift,

00:00:23.521 --> 00:00:28.084
the semantically meaningful changes in how data are
created, collected,

00:00:28.164 --> 00:00:31.158
and curated that actually drive those failures,

00:00:31.238 --> 00:00:37.437
and lays out three concrete ways to address it:
leaning on human intuition and expert knowledge,

00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:42.080
building dynamic benchmarks, and clearly stating a
model's limitations.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.010
Benchmark datasets play two roles: they let
researchers compare methods,

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.741
and they stand in as an imperfect model of the real world.

00:00:50.821 --> 00:00:56.014
The trouble is that a single static benchmark can
never fully capture the dynamic,

00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:58.715
high-dimensional complexity of real tasks.

00:00:58.795 --> 00:01:04.375
So models that appear to match or beat human-level
accuracy on a benchmark routinely err

00:01:04.455 --> 00:01:08.684
on out-of-distribution and adversarially perturbed
production data.

00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:11.963
The field describes this gap as distribution shift,

00:01:12.043 --> 00:01:17.366
with subcategories like covariate shift, prior
probability shift, and concept shift.

00:01:17.446 --> 00:01:23.218
But these definitions are over-specified for
comparing two data samples and under-specified

00:01:23.298 --> 00:01:28.170
for evaluating the data-generating process that
actually drives the mismatch.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.164
Prior work treats robustness as a difference
between two distributions while disregarding

00:01:32.244 --> 00:01:35.858
the reasons behind that difference.

00:01:35.938 --> 00:01:39.552
But the difference is merely a symptom; the disease
is in how data are created,

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:43.246
collected, and curated.

00:01:43.326 --> 00:01:46.940
As Figure 1 shows,

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:50.634
interest in distribution shift terminology has
grown enormously over the last decade,

00:01:50.714 --> 00:01:54.328
with covariate shift papers alone climbing toward
nine thousand per year by 2021.

00:01:54.408 --> 00:01:58.022
Despite this volume,

00:01:58.102 --> 00:02:01.716
the vocabulary stays tangled and rarely points
practitioners toward the upstream,

00:02:01.796 --> 00:02:05.410
semantically meaningful factors they can actually
intervene on.

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:09.859
This is a perspective paper that reframes the
robustness problem.

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:13.498
Its central contribution is the concept of context shift,

00:02:13.578 --> 00:02:18.860
which describes the semantically meaningful
upstream factors, sample selection bias,

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.861
adversarial perturbation, and non-stationarity,

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.797
that drive the distribution shifts we observe.

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:26.265
On top of that framing,

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:31.628
Groh identifies three practical methods for closing
the benchmark-to-production gap,

00:02:31.708 --> 00:02:38.139
and illustrates all of them through three recurring
case-study domains: facial expression recognition,

00:02:38.219 --> 00:02:40.820
deepfake detection, and medical diagnosis.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.197
Context refers to the semantically meaningful
upstream factors that drive a distribution shift.

00:02:45.277 --> 00:02:49.274
Rather than fixating on the difference between two
distributions,

00:02:49.354 --> 00:02:53.351
Groh asks researchers to reason about the three
processes that generate that difference: sample selection bias,

00:02:53.431 --> 00:02:57.428
for example a new dataset drawn from a demographic
absent from the old one;

00:02:57.508 --> 00:03:01.505
adversarial perturbations, imperceptible noise
injections that change model behavior;

00:03:01.585 --> 00:03:05.582
and non-stationarity, where the world itself moves on,

00:03:05.662 --> 00:03:09.659
like smartphones after 2018 versus flip phones
before 2010.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:13.736
Three methods follow.

00:03:13.816 --> 00:03:17.814
First,

00:03:17.894 --> 00:03:21.891
human intuition and expert knowledge can identify
the semantically meaningful features

00:03:21.971 --> 00:03:25.968
on which models are likely to fail.

00:03:26.048 --> 00:03:30.045
Second,

00:03:30.125 --> 00:03:34.122
dynamic benchmarking replaces a single static test
set with continual evaluation against a well-specified,

00:03:34.202 --> 00:03:38.199
quality-controlled data-generation process,
producing corroborated accuracy,

00:03:38.279 --> 00:03:42.276
the distribution of scores across many benchmarks.

00:03:42.356 --> 00:03:46.353
Third, authors should clarify a model's limitations,

00:03:46.433 --> 00:03:50.430
explicitly stating the contexts in which
performance is known and unknown.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.363
Rather than introducing a new dataset,

00:03:53.443 --> 00:03:57.520
the paper dissects existing benchmarks across three
domains.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.816
In facial expression recognition it examines seven
benchmarks,

00:04:01.896 --> 00:04:04.449
from SFEW and MMI to CK+ and MultiPie.

00:04:04.529 --> 00:04:09.576
In deepfake detection it centers on the DFDC
dataset, the largest to date,

00:04:09.656 --> 00:04:16.020
with over one hundred twenty-eight thousand videos
from nine hundred sixty consenting actors.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:23.641
In medical diagnosis it looks at store-and-forward
teledermatology and the Diverse Dermatology Images dataset.

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:28.353
Throughout, it holds up context-aware benchmarks
like BREEDS, WILDS,

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:35.420
and Dynabench as models for capturing the
data-generation process instead of a single static snapshot.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.082
The headline evidence is how wildly performance
swings with the benchmark.

00:04:40.162 --> 00:04:44.443
One facial expression model, built on AlexNet,

00:04:44.523 --> 00:04:48.805
ranges from just 48.6 percent accuracy on SFEW to
94.8 percent on MultiPie,

00:04:48.885 --> 00:04:53.167
against a random baseline of 14.2 percent,

00:04:53.247 --> 00:04:57.528
so which benchmark you pick determines the story you tell.

00:04:57.608 --> 00:05:01.890
The pattern generalizes: when researchers
faithfully recreated the ImageNet and CIFAR-10 test sets,

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:06.252
state-of-the-art accuracy fell by three to fifteen
percent.

00:05:06.332 --> 00:05:10.613
And a leading DFDC deepfake detector assigned only
two and eight percent likelihood to

00:05:10.693 --> 00:05:14.975
genuine deepfakes of Kim Jong-un and Vladimir Putin,

00:05:15.055 --> 00:05:19.337
precisely the high-context videos that matter in
the real world.

00:05:19.417 --> 00:05:23.698
In dermatology,

00:05:23.778 --> 00:05:28.060
benchmarks that barely include dark skin let
systematic errors hide in plain sight.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.045
A few numbers anchor the argument.

00:05:31.125 --> 00:05:38.389
A single facial expression model spans 48.6 to 94.8
percent accuracy across seven benchmarks,

00:05:38.469 --> 00:05:41.469
next to a 14.2 percent random baseline.

00:05:41.549 --> 00:05:50.234
Faithfully recreated ImageNet and CIFAR-10 test
sets cut state-of-the-art accuracy by three to fifteen percent.

00:05:50.314 --> 00:05:58.605
A leading deepfake detector gave only two and eight
percent likelihood to real deepfakes of world leaders.

00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:03.027
The DFDC benchmark holds 128,154 videos from 960 actors.

00:06:03.107 --> 00:06:09.976
And one dermatology benchmark included just 2.7
percent of the second-darkest skin type,

00:06:10.056 --> 00:06:12.740
and a single person of the darkest.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.373
The lasting message is a shift in perspective: stop
treating the benchmark-to-production

00:06:17.453 --> 00:06:21.707
gap as a mere statistical distance between two
datasets and start treating it as context

00:06:21.787 --> 00:06:26.040
shift,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:30.373
the semantically meaningful changes in how data are
generated.

00:06:30.453 --> 00:06:34.707
Address it by pairing machine learning with human
intuition and expertise,

00:06:34.787 --> 00:06:39.040
by moving from static to dynamic benchmarks that
evaluate the data-generation process,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.373
and by clearly clarifying each model's limitations.

00:06:43.453 --> 00:06:47.707
The simplest and most immediately effective step is
honesty: state plainly the contexts

00:06:47.787 --> 00:06:52.040
in which a model has been evaluated and the
contexts in which its behavior remains unknown.
